\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Leaderboards hold a matrix of models by benchmarks with many holes: most models were run on a few of the benchmarks, and an evaluator with a new model can afford to run only $k$ of the $N$. Three questions follow. Which $k$ benchmarks should be run? How should the model's scores on the others be inferred from them and from the historical matrix? And how large must $k$ be before the answer is good enough? The first two are the cold-start problem of recommender systems and item selection in adaptive testing, whose machinery (a probabilistic factor model, a greedy optimal-design criterion) is classical, and we do not claim a new selection method. What is missing for benchmarks is the answer to the third question under the protocol that matches the use: whole new models, grouped by the organisation that made them, are held out, everything is fitted on the other models, and the error is measured on benchmarks that no selection rule could have chosen.

The last condition matters: if each rule is scored on the benchmarks it leaves unrun, the target changes with the rule and with $k$. We therefore split the benchmarks at random into a pool from which a rule may choose and a target set it may not, repeat this over 50 splits, and report the excess over each model's own error when all of the pool is given, its floor (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}). Margins for what counts as a difference were set before any error was computed, from the gaps between adjacent models; they proved smaller than anything attainable, so we add a coarser second margin and say which was pre-set.

On 238 embedding models and 328 tasks of the Massive Text Embedding Benchmark~\citep{muennighoff2023mteb}, with whole organisations held out, we find the following (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}); the same protocol on the per-subtask results of the Open LLM Leaderboard, 4{,}507 open language models and 39 subtasks, says which of these findings carry over.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{How many.} The answer is a curve, not one number. Choosing the benchmarks by the factor model's design criterion, the error on the target set is within 2 points of the floor at about 8 benchmarks and within 1 point at 50 (the interval reaches 32); the floor itself is about 5 points, and the pre-set margin of about 0.1 point, which would resolve neighbouring models, is not reached at any $k\le109$. On the leaderboard the same excess is reached at 2 to 3 and at 5 to 13 of 39 benchmarks.
\item \emph{Which.} Designed selection is clearly better than random selection when very few benchmarks can be run (1.9 points of RMSE at $k=2$ and $3$ on MTEB; 1.4 to 2.1 points at $k\le3$ for held-out families and 2.5 to 3.3 for the strongest quarter) and practically equivalent from about 8 on MTEB and from 5 to 8 on the leaderboard; the benchmarks most often run are worse than random.
\item \emph{Why the error is where it is.} A closed-form risk of the factor model, which we derive, splits the error into noise, a floor misfit, a design term and a remainder (Section~\ref{sec:theory}). It predicts the design term and the gain from design well on MTEB and for held-out families of the leaderboard, but the measured floor on MTEB is 2.5 times what the model predicts, about half of that is extrapolation to new organisations, and none of the misspecifications we simulate reproduces it. On the leaderboard the ratio is 1.1 for held-out families and 2.5 for the strongest quarter, so the misfit is not a property of benchmarks in general, and the risk is a decomposition and not a rule that gives $k$ from the spectrum.
\item \emph{How to choose $k$.} A rule that extrapolates $k^*$ from the excess curve on the training models alone is within one grid step of the measured $k^*$ when whole groups are held out, on MTEB (set after interim results) and on the leaderboard (set in advance); it fails under a shift in strength or time.
\item \emph{Scope.} Thinner data or fewer training models raise the floor and leave the gain from design at 4--8\% of it at $k=20$.
\end{itemize}
